```
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Pair;
public class FindPair {
 public static Pair findPair(int[] nums, int targetSum) {
 Map map = new HashMap<>();
 for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
 int complement = targetSum - nums[i];
 if (map.containsKey(complement)) {
 return new Pair(complement, nums[i]);
 }
 map.put(nums[i], i);
 }
 return null;
 }
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 int[] nums = {2, 7, 11, 15];
 int targetSum = 9;
 Pair pair = findPair(nums, targetSum);
 if (pair != null) {
 System.out.println("Pair found: " + pair.getKey() + ", " + pair.getValue());
 } else {
 System.out.println("Pair not found.");
 }
 }
}
```
This code uses a hash table to store the elements of the array and their indices. It iterates through the array and checks if the complement of the target sum (i.e. the difference between the target sum and the current element) is already in the hash table. If it is, it returns a pair of the complement and the current element. If not, it adds the current element to the hash table.
The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), where n is the number of elements in the array, since it only needs to iterate through the array once. The space complexity is also O(n), since the hash table can potentially store all elements of the array.

